= Revised Plan
After receiving feedback for our proposal, we realised that our initial scope was too large and presented both time
constraint challenges as well as domain knowledge limits. We simply do not have enough time to implement more than 3
algorithms as well as ensuring their validity across varying network topolgies and latencies. Furthermore, we would have
needed to spend far more time researching leader election algorithms to be able to fully understand the theoretical
effects of changing various experimental variables. Thus we chose to narrow our scope to reduce the number of
independent variables and configurations so we could complete the project. Reducing the scope also means we are able to
more thoroughly investigate and understand the algorithms that fall under our new scope, creating a more refined result.

Starting week 8, we will test our algorithms. We expect some debugging will be necessary during this phase, and will not
collect final raw results until the Monday of week 9 at the latest, targeting all testing be completed by the end of the
weekend prior. An allocation of around a week to finalise our implementation is appropriate as we still face the
challenge of ensuring code correctness. For the collection of these results, we assume that our algorithms will run at
reasonable speeds such that collection of the data will take at most 2 days. Additionally, the creation of our testing
framework should ensure our tests can be automated to allow us to allocate more time to other tasks.

Furthermore, as part of our reduction of scope, we have opted to avoid testing on an actual simulated network. While
this reduces the validity of our results, the testing framework we have created works best on a single process emulating
a large network, rather than directly distributing the simulation across multiple processes. Reducing our scope in such
a manner also allows us to spend more time ensuring the simulator is bug-free, as the additional complications of actual
network testing would significantly increase the pre-experiment workload to the point that the project would be
unfeasible.

While three group members are managing the codebase and collecting results, our remaining two group members will begin
revising our final report for presentation as well as begin designing the early layouts of the poster.

Once our data is collected by Monday of week 9, we will begin analysing the results with the goal of completing this by
Friday of week 9. With the remaining time, we hope to finalise our report and create our poster using elements from our
report. Depending on the exact due date of our project in week 10, we can condense other parts or work on tasks in
parallel to finish them earlier.

We believe this revised plan will allow us to finish our project on time, while also creating a more focused output.
Also, we plan to do things in parallel to speed up completion of tasks that do not depend on each other.
